# The settings for AAP behind STIG Manager's nginx with the Keycloak card login (docs/AAP_KEYCLOAK.md).
#
# One file for the three scripts. Copy it, put your names in, and hand it to each with --settings:
#   cp tools/aap-keycloak.env.example aap-keycloak.env
#   tools/aap-nginx-blocks.py   --settings aap-keycloak.env     writes the nginx blocks to ./out
#   tools/aap-keycloak-realm.py --settings aap-keycloak.env     prints what it would make in Keycloak
#   tools/aap-keycloak-check.sh --settings aap-keycloak.env     checks the chain, read-only
#
# NAME=value lines. A line that starts with # is not read. No password belongs in this file: a password is in
# a file of its own (mode 600) that a line here names, or it is asked for at the prompt.
# Each script's --help explains every setting. The ones without a line here keep their default.
#
# ONE FILE PER NETWORK. Each network (each enclave) gets a settings file of its own, for example
# aap-keycloak.enclave-a.env. Every address, DN, CA file and realm name the scripts use comes from that file:
# nothing of a network is written in a script. A settings file is yours and never goes into Git (.gitignore
# holds aap-keycloak*.env, so an update of this folder leaves it alone too). Carry no settings file from one
# network to another: copy this example there and fill it in (the ENCLAVE part at the end lists what to ask for).

# --- the names ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The address people type for AAP. https, a name of its own, no path.
AAP_URL=https://aap.yoursite.mil
# The AAP gateway itself: the AAP host and the gateway's port (guide, step 1.2).
AAP_UPSTREAM=https://aap01.yoursite.mil:443
# Keycloak's address as people's browsers use it, without /realms/...
KEYCLOAK_URL=https://keycloak.yoursite.mil
# STIG Manager's own address.
STIGMAN_URL=https://stigman.yoursite.mil
# Your CA chain (PEM), when this machine's trust store does not have it.
#CA_FILE=/home/you/your-ca-chain.pem

# --- nginx (tools/aap-nginx-blocks.py) ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The nginx container's name, and its listen line as STIG Manager's own server block has it (guide, step 1.3).
NGINX_CONTAINER=stigman-nginx
NGINX_LISTEN=443 ssl
# What Keycloak's address needs: lines (two lines for the location nginx already has), server (a whole server
# block, when Keycloak is not behind this nginx yet) or off.
KEYCLOAK_NGINX=lines
#KEYCLOAK_UPSTREAM=http://keycloak:8080
#NGINX_CLIENT_CA=/etc/nginx/tls/dod-ca-chain.pem
# The report website: the folder report_web_dest writes to, as the STIG Manager host sees it.
# With a line here nginx serves it at STIGMAN_URL/reports/, behind STIG Manager's card check.
#REPORT_WEB_FOLDER=/srv/site-reports
# No restart of nginx: a web server that already serves that folder, in place of REPORT_WEB_FOLDER.
#REPORTS_UPSTREAM=http://web01.yoursite.mil:8080

# --- Keycloak (tools/aap-keycloak-realm.py) ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The NEW realm for AAP. Never the realm STIG Manager uses.
KEYCLOAK_REALM=aap
# Realms the script refuses to work in. Put STIG Manager's realm here beside master.
KEYCLOAK_PROTECTED_REALMS=master,stigman
# The admin account, and a file (mode 600) with its password. No file: the script asks at the prompt.
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=admin
#KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD_FILE=/home/you/keycloak-admin.pw
# Where the script reaches the admin API when that is not KEYCLOAK_URL. On the Keycloak host: its own port.
#KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_URL=http://localhost:8080
# The Keycloak container's name, for the kcadm way (on the Keycloak host).
#KEYCLOAK_CONTAINER=keycloak
# The client, and the groups AAP's maps name. (With LDAP_URL no group is made by default: the groups then
# come from the directory, LDAP_TEAM_GROUPS.)
KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=aap
#AAP_GROUPS=aap-admins,aap-operators,aap-users
# What in the card's certificate names the person: cn, edipi, upn or email (guide, step 4.3).
# Default: cn - and upn with LDAP_URL (the card's Principal Name, Active Directory's userPrincipalName).
#X509_IDENTITY=cn
# The people, from a list - only without a directory (guide, step 4.8).
#AAP_USERS_FILE=/home/you/aap-people.csv

# --- the people, from Active Directory (guide, "People from Active Directory") -----------------------------
# A READ-ONLY federation: Keycloak reads the directory and never writes to it. Ask the directory's admins for
# the five things below. The bind account's password is in a file of its own (mode 600), or asked at the prompt.
#LDAP_URL=ldaps://dc01.corp.example.mil:636
# The CA chain (PEM) that signed the directory server's certificate. Keycloak's own trust store needs it too.
#LDAP_CA_FILE=/home/you/ad-ca-chain.pem
#LDAP_USERS_DN=OU=People,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=mil
#LDAP_BIND_DN=CN=svc-keycloak,OU=Service,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=mil
#LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD_FILE=/home/you/ldap-bind.pw
# The one group whose members may log in to AAP (the group your identity manager fills). Nobody else is let in.
#LDAP_GROUP_DN=CN=AAP-Access,OU=Groups,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=mil
# The directory's groups that become AAP's teams, full DNs with ; between them. AAP's maps name them by their CN.
#LDAP_TEAM_GROUPS=CN=AAP-Admins,OU=Groups,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=mil;CN=AAP-Operators,OU=Groups,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=mil
# The directory attribute that holds what the card gives (the card's Principal Name). Default: userPrincipalName.
#LDAP_CARD_ATTRIBUTE=userPrincipalName
# How often Keycloak reads the directory, in seconds: the changed people, and all of them.
#LDAP_SYNC_CHANGED_SECONDS=900
#LDAP_SYNC_FULL_SECONDS=86400

# --- the check (tools/aap-keycloak-check.sh) ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The CAs of people's cards (PEM): a copy of the file nginx's ssl_client_certificate line names. The check's
# "Card CAs" line compares it with what nginx asks for, and names this file.
#CARD_CA_FILE=/home/you/card-ca-chain.pem
# A file (mode 600) with one AAP token of an admin or auditor, for the "Token" and "AAP login" lines.
#AAP_TOKEN_FILE=/home/you/aap.token
# The person whose tokens the "Groups" line looks at (a Keycloak user name). Default: the first one listed.
#CHECK_USER=jdoe

# --- ENCLAVE: what is this network's own -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Another network (a classified enclave, an air-gapped one) is another file: aap-keycloak.enclave-a.env.
# Nothing is carried over from the first network but this example. On that network, ask for each of these and
# put it in the lines above (guide, "Building it in an air-gapped enclave"):
#
#   the names        AAP_URL, AAP_UPSTREAM, KEYCLOAK_URL, STIGMAN_URL - that network's DNS names
#   the realm        KEYCLOAK_REALM, and STIG Manager's realm there in KEYCLOAK_PROTECTED_REALMS
#   the server CA    CA_FILE - the chain that signed that network's server certificates
#   the card CA      CARD_CA_FILE, NGINX_CLIENT_CA - that network's tokens chain to ANOTHER PKI than the
#                    unclassified cards. Never reuse the first network's chain.
#   the directory    LDAP_URL, LDAP_CA_FILE, LDAP_USERS_DN, LDAP_BIND_DN, LDAP_GROUP_DN, LDAP_TEAM_GROUPS -
#                    that network's domain, its own bind account, its own groups
#   the card's name  X509_IDENTITY, LDAP_CARD_ATTRIBUTE - the Principal Name on that network's tokens can be
#                    written another way: look at one token and one directory account first
#   the passwords    KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD_FILE, LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD_FILE - files made there, never carried.
#                    The client's secret is made there too, by --apply --secret-file.
#
# The scripts call nothing outside the addresses in this file. They need no internet.
